NNAAMMEE
       nsenter - run program in different namespaces

SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
       nnsseenntteerr [options] [_p_r_o_g_r_a_m [_a_r_g_u_m_e_n_t_s]]

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
       The nnsseenntteerr command executes _p_r_o_g_r_a_m in the namespace(s) that are
       specified in the command-line options (described below). If _p_r_o_g_r_a_m is
       not given, then "${SHELL}" is run (default: _/_b_i_n_/_s_h).

       Enterable namespaces are:

       mmoouunntt nnaammeessppaaccee
           Mounting and unmounting filesystems will not affect the rest of the
           system, except for filesystems which are explicitly marked as
           shared (with mmoouunntt ----mmaakkee--sshhaarreedd; see _/_p_r_o_c_/_s_e_l_f_/_m_o_u_n_t_i_n_f_o for the
           sshhaarreedd flag). For further details, see mmoouunntt__nnaammeessppaacceess(7) and the
           discussion of the CCLLOONNEE__NNEEWWNNSS flag in cclloonnee(2).

       UUTTSS nnaammeessppaaccee
           Setting hostname or domainname will not affect the rest of the
           system. For further details, see uuttss__nnaammeessppaacceess(7).

       IIPPCC nnaammeessppaaccee
           The process will have an independent namespace for POSIX message
           queues as well as System V message queues, semaphore sets and
           shared memory segments. For further details, see iippcc__nnaammeessppaacceess(7).

       nneettwwoorrkk nnaammeessppaaccee
           The process will have independent IPv4 and IPv6 stacks, IP routing
           tables, firewall rules, the _/_p_r_o_c_/_n_e_t and _/_s_y_s_/_c_l_a_s_s_/_n_e_t directory
           trees, sockets, etc. For further details, see
           nneettwwoorrkk__nnaammeessppaacceess(7).

       PPIIDD nnaammeessppaaccee
           Children will have a set of PID to process mappings separate from
           the nnsseenntteerr process. nnsseenntteerr will fork by default if changing the
           PID namespace, so that the new program and its children share the
           same PID namespace and are visible to each other. If ----nnoo--ffoorrkk is
           used, the new program will be exec’ed without forking. For further
           details, see ppiidd__nnaammeessppaacceess(7).

       uusseerr nnaammeessppaaccee
           The process will have a distinct set of UIDs, GIDs and
           capabilities. For further details, see uusseerr__nnaammeessppaacceess(7).

       ccggrroouupp nnaammeessppaaccee
           The process will have a virtualized view of _/_p_r_o_c_/_s_e_l_f_/_c_g_r_o_u_p, and
           new cgroup mounts will be rooted at the namespace cgroup root. For
           further details, see ccggrroouupp__nnaammeessppaacceess(7).

       ttiimmee nnaammeessppaaccee
           The process can have a distinct view of CCLLOOCCKK__MMOONNOOTTOONNIICC and/or
           CCLLOOCCKK__BBOOOOTTTTIIMMEE which can be changed using
           _/_p_r_o_c_/_s_e_l_f_/_t_i_m_e_n_s___o_f_f_s_e_t_s. For further details, see
           ttiimmee__nnaammeessppaacceess(7).

OOPPTTIIOONNSS
       Various of the options below that relate to namespaces take an optional
       _f_i_l_e argument. This should be one of the _/_p_r_o_c_/_[_p_i_d_]_/_n_s_/_* files
       described in nnaammeessppaacceess(7), or the pathname of a bind mount that was
       created on one of those files.

       --aa, ----aallll
           Enter all namespaces of the target process by the default
           _/_p_r_o_c_/_[_p_i_d_]_/_n_s_/_* namespace paths. The default paths to the target
           process namespaces may be overwritten by namespace specific options
           (e.g., ----aallll ----mmoouunntt=[_p_a_t_h]).

           The user namespace will be ignored if the same as the caller’s
           current user namespace. It prevents a caller that has dropped
           capabilities from regaining those capabilities via a call to
           setns(). See sseettnnss(2) for more details.

       --tt, ----ttaarrggeett _P_I_D
           Specify a target process to get contexts from. The paths to the
           contexts specified by _p_i_d are:

           _/_p_r_o_c_/_p_i_d_/_n_s_/_m_n_t
               the mount namespace

           _/_p_r_o_c_/_p_i_d_/_n_s_/_u_t_s
               the UTS namespace

           _/_p_r_o_c_/_p_i_d_/_n_s_/_i_p_c
               the IPC namespace

           _/_p_r_o_c_/_p_i_d_/_n_s_/_n_e_t
               the network namespace

           _/_p_r_o_c_/_p_i_d_/_n_s_/_p_i_d
               the PID namespace

           _/_p_r_o_c_/_p_i_d_/_n_s_/_u_s_e_r
               the user namespace

           _/_p_r_o_c_/_p_i_d_/_n_s_/_c_g_r_o_u_p
               the cgroup namespace

           _/_p_r_o_c_/_p_i_d_/_n_s_/_t_i_m_e
               the time namespace

           _/_p_r_o_c_/_p_i_d_/_r_o_o_t
               the root directory

           _/_p_r_o_c_/_p_i_d_/_c_w_d
               the working directory respectively

       --mm, ----mmoouunntt[=_f_i_l_e]
           Enter the mount namespace. If no file is specified, enter the mount
           namespace of the target process. If _f_i_l_e is specified, enter the
           mount namespace specified by _f_i_l_e.

       --uu, ----uuttss[=_f_i_l_e]
           Enter the UTS namespace. If no file is specified, enter the UTS
           namespace of the target process. If _f_i_l_e is specified, enter the
           UTS namespace specified by _f_i_l_e.

       --ii, ----iippcc[=_f_i_l_e]
           Enter the IPC namespace. If no file is specified, enter the IPC
           namespace of the target process. If _f_i_l_e is specified, enter the
           IPC namespace specified by _f_i_l_e.

       --nn, ----nneett[=_f_i_l_e]
           Enter the network namespace. If no file is specified, enter the
           network namespace of the target process. If _f_i_l_e is specified,
           enter the network namespace specified by _f_i_l_e.

       --pp, ----ppiidd[=_f_i_l_e]
           Enter the PID namespace. If no file is specified, enter the PID
           namespace of the target process. If _f_i_l_e is specified, enter the
           PID namespace specified by _f_i_l_e.

       --UU, ----uusseerr[=_f_i_l_e]
           Enter the user namespace. If no file is specified, enter the user
           namespace of the target process. If _f_i_l_e is specified, enter the
           user namespace specified by _f_i_l_e. See also the ----sseettuuiidd and
           ----sseettggiidd options.

       --CC, ----ccggrroouupp[=_f_i_l_e]
           Enter the cgroup namespace. If no file is specified, enter the
           cgroup namespace of the target process. If _f_i_l_e is specified, enter
           the cgroup namespace specified by _f_i_l_e.

       --TT, ----ttiimmee[=_f_i_l_e]
           Enter the time namespace. If no file is specified, enter the time
           namespace of the target process. If _f_i_l_e is specified, enter the
           time namespace specified by _f_i_l_e.

       --GG, ----sseettggiidd _g_i_d
           Set the group ID which will be used in the entered namespace and
           drop supplementary groups. nnsseenntteerr always sets GID for user
           namespaces, the default is 0.

       --SS, ----sseettuuiidd _u_i_d
           Set the user ID which will be used in the entered namespace.
           nnsseenntteerr always sets UID for user namespaces, the default is 0.

       ----pprreesseerrvvee--ccrreeddeennttiiaallss
           Don’t modify UID and GID when enter user namespace. The default is
           to drops supplementary groups and sets GID and UID to 0.

       --rr, ----rroooott[=_d_i_r_e_c_t_o_r_y]
           Set the root directory. If no directory is specified, set the root
           directory to the root directory of the target process. If directory
           is specified, set the root directory to the specified directory.
           The specified _d_i_r_e_c_t_o_r_y is open before it switches to the requested
           namespaces.

       --ww, ----wwdd[=_d_i_r_e_c_t_o_r_y]
           Set the working directory. If no directory is specified, set the
           working directory to the working directory of the target process.
           If directory is specified, set the working directory to the
           specified directory. The specified _d_i_r_e_c_t_o_r_y is open before it
           switches to the requested namespaces, it means the specified
           directory works as "tunnel" to the current namespace. See also
           ----wwddnnss.

       --WW, ----wwddnnss[=_d_i_r_e_c_t_o_r_y]
           Set the working directory. The _d_i_r_e_c_t_o_r_y is open after switch to
           the requested namespaces and after cchhrroooott(2) call. The options ----wwdd
           and ----wwddnnss are mutually exclusive.

       --FF, ----nnoo--ffoorrkk
           Do not fork before exec’ing the specified program. By default, when
           entering a PID namespace, nnsseenntteerr calls ffoorrkk before calling eexxeecc so
           that any children will also be in the newly entered PID namespace.

       --ZZ, ----ffoollllooww--ccoonntteexxtt
           Set the SELinux security context used for executing a new process
           according to already running process specified by ----ttaarrggeett PID.
           (The util-linux has to be compiled with SELinux support otherwise
           the option is unavailable.)

       --hh, ----hheellpp
           Display help text and exit.

       --VV, ----vveerrssiioonn
           Print version and exit.

